Ladybird now lays out text on a path as SVG 2’s text layout algorithm
does: Each glyph sits at the point of the middle of its advance, turned
to the direction of the path there, with its baseline shift and any dy
applied at a right angle to the path, and a glyph whose middle is off
either end of the path is hidden.

Blink does the same in SvgTextLayoutAlgorithm::PositionOnPath(), which
samples PathPositionMapper::PointAndNormalAtLength() at the middle of
each glyph, and hides the glyphs that are off the path. Gecko does it in
SVGTextFrame::DoTextPathLayout(), and WebKit in the position-on-path
step of its SVGTextLayoutAlgorithm. Each of the three turns a glyph to
the tangent at its middle, rather than to the line through the start and
the end of its advance that the spec describes.

The new reftest’s page and its reference render identically in Chrome
158.0.8127.36, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 (engine-compare).
